# The loop stores doubles, so new Array(count) makes storage for unboxed doubles. A store is a test of the index against the length, and the store. The number is a product of two integers: it is finite, so it is no NaN (which marks a hole), and nothing asks it.
# CHECK-LABEL: steps by 1
# CHECK: Int32 b@[[k]] = Phi(
# CHECK-NEXT: Double b@[[wide]] = IToD(b@[[k]],
# CHECK-NEXT: Double b@[[square]] = Mul(b@[[wide]], b@[[wide]],
# CHECK-NOT: Equal(b@[[square]], b@[[square]]
# CHECK: Int32 b@[[isInside]] = Above(b@[[length]], b@[[k]], {{*}}:PutArrayElement)
# CHECK-NEXT: Branch(b@[[isInside]], Terminal,
# CHECK-NEXT: Successors: Then:#[[store]], Else:Unlikely:#
# CHECK-NEXT: BB#[[store]]:
# CHECK: Store(b@[[square]], b@{{*}}, ControlDependent|Writes:indexedDoubleProperties, {{*}}:PutArrayElement)
# CHECK: Branch(b@{{*}}, Terminal, {{*}}:Less)
# CHECK-LABEL: end of loop
# The storage and the length are read ahead of the loop, nothing is boxed, and the kind of storage is not tested in it.
# NONE-LABEL: steps by 1
# NONE-NOT: Load(
# NONE-NOT: BitwiseCast(
# NONE-LABEL: end of loop
# CALLS: f1: size=
# CALLS: stub NewArrayWithLength.Double x1
# CALLS-NOT: stub NewArray
# CALLS: f{{*}}: size=
# One loop, a turn at a time: each turn keeps the test of the index and polls.
# X64: loop #{{*}}, instructions: 7, patchpoints: 1, polls: 1, {{*}} steps by 1
# ARM64: loop #{{*}}, instructions: 6, patchpoints: 1, polls: 1, {{*}} steps by 1
